16:["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 pt-3 border-t border-amber-200 text-gray-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Your task: "}],"Learn the modifiers that tell a payer the default assumption does not apply here."]}]
17:["$","p","1",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"A modifier is a two-character code appended to a CPT or HCPCS code. It does not change what the code means; it adds a fact about the circumstances — that the service was distinct, that it was on the left side, that it was reduced, that a different provider performed it."}]
18:["$","figure","2",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200","children":["$","table",null,{"className":"w-full text-left border-collapse","children":[["$","thead",null,{"children":["$","tr",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50","children":[["$","th","0",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Modifier"}],["$","th","1",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Means"}],["$","th","2",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Typical use"}]]}]}],["$","tbody",null,{"children":[["$","tr","0",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"25"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Significant, separately identifiable E/M service on the same day as a procedure"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"A patient seen for one thing who is also evaluated for something else"}]]}],["$","tr","1",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"59"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Distinct procedural service"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Two procedures normally bundled that were genuinely separate here"}]]}],["$","tr","2",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"LT / RT"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Left side / right side"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Procedures on paired structures"}]]}],["$","tr","3",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"50"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Bilateral procedure"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"The same procedure on both sides"}]]}],["$","tr","4",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"76"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Repeat procedure by the same provider"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"A second X-ray the same day"}]]}],["$","tr","5",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"91"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Repeat clinical diagnostic laboratory test"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"A repeated lab test for a clinical reason"}]]}],["$","tr","6",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"GA"}],"$L20","$L21"]}]]}]]}]}],"$L22"]}]
19:["$","p","3",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Modifier 25 and modifier 59 are the two most misused codes in the entire field, and both are routinely audited. The distinction is worth stating precisely: modifier 25 applies to an evaluation and management service performed alongside a procedure. Modifier 59 applies to a procedure performed alongside another procedure."}]
1a:["$","div","4",{"className":"p-4 rounded-xl border-l-4 text-gray-800 leading-relaxed bg-yellow-50 border-yellow-400","children":[false,"Modifier 59 in particular is an audit flag. It overrides a National Correct Coding Initiative edit — a payer rule saying two codes should not normally be billed together — so appending it asserts that this case is a genuine exception. Where a more specific modifier exists (XE, XS, XP, XU), use it instead. Never append 59 simply to get a denied claim paid; doing so knowingly is unbundling, which is fraud rather than an aggressive coding choice."]}]
1b:["$","div","5",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-primary-200 bg-primary-50/50 p-6","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-primary-700 mb-2","children":"Worked example"}],["$","h4",null,{"className":"text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1","children":"Deciding whether modifier 25 applies"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-5","children":"A patient attends for a scheduled joint injection and also raises a new, unrelated complaint that the provider evaluates."}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"space-y-4","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":1}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Ask whether the E/M work was significant and separately identifiable."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"This is the actual test. Every procedure includes some evaluation — assessing the site, confirming the plan. Modifier 25 is for work beyond that, addressing a different issue. Routine pre-procedure assessment never qualifies."}]]}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":2}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Check the documentation supports a distinct evaluation."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"There must be a separately documented history, assessment, and plan for the other complaint. If the note describes only the injection, the E/M is not supportable regardless of what happened in the room — and the fix is a provider query, not a modifier."}]]}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":3}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Append modifier 25 to the E/M code, not to the procedure."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"This trips people constantly. The modifier goes on the code it describes, and modifier 25 describes the E/M service. Appending it to the injection code is simply wrong and will be rejected."}]]}]]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":4}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Confirm a different diagnosis supports the E/M where possible."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"A separate diagnosis code linked to the E/M makes the separate identifiability visible to the payer on the face of the claim. It is not strictly required, but it substantially reduces the chance of a denial you then have to appeal."}]]}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-5 pt-4 border-t border-primary-200 text-gray-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Result: "}],"The injection and the office visit both paid, with the E/M carrying modifier 25 and its own supporting diagnosis."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-gray-900 font-medium","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","children":"🔑 "}],"Modifier 25 attaches to the E/M, and only when the evaluation went genuinely beyond the procedure's inherent assessment."]}]]}]
1c:["$","$L23","6",{"block":{"type":"knowledge-check","question":"A provider performs a scheduled procedure and, at the same visit, evaluates a separate new complaint. Which modifier applies, and to which code?","options":["Modifier 59, appended to the procedure code","Modifier 25, appended to the E/M code","Modifier 25, appended to the procedure code","No modifier — the visit is included in the procedure"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Modifier 25 identifies a significant, separately identifiable E/M service on the same day as a procedure, and it attaches to the E/M code it describes. Modifier 59 applies between two procedures, not between a procedure and an E/M service.","optionRationales":["Modifier 59 distinguishes one procedure from another. There is only one procedure here.","Correct. Modifier 25 goes on the E/M code.","The modifier attaches to the code it describes, which is the E/M service, not the procedure.","Evaluating a separate new complaint is exactly what modifier 25 exists for. Omitting it forfeits legitimate payment."]}}]
1d:["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-12","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3","children":"Challenge"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl","children":"Apply what you've learned in this lesson."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-md border border-gray-200 p-8","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Modifiers reward precision. Work from the CPT modifier appendix."}],["$","ol","1",{"start":"$undefined","className":"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-700 marker:font-semibold marker:text-primary-600","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Find the modifier appendix in your CPT book and read the full descriptions of modifiers 25, 59, 50, and 76."}],["$","li","1",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Research the X modifiers (XE, XS, XP, XU) and explain in three sentences why CMS introduced them as alternatives to 59."}],["$","li","2",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"For a bilateral procedure, determine whether your payer prefers modifier 50 or LT and RT on separate lines. Note where you found the answer."}],["$","li","3",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Write a short guidance note for a colleague on when modifier 25 is and is not appropriate, using the injection scenario as the example."}]]}]]}]}]]}]
